  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nHot and humid (at least for this region) conditions are forecast\nfor the latter half of the week as the subtropical ridge will\nstrengthen once again with peak H5 heights likely reaching \n597-599dm over northern Arizona, southern Nevada, and southern \nUtah. The strength looks to be just shy of what just occurred this\npast weekend, but guidance shows the ridge center to our north \ninstead of directly over central Arizona. \n\nAfternoon temperatures Wednesday are forecast to drop into the\n107-111 degree range across the lower deserts, resulting in \nvery localized coverage of Major HeatRisk. However, we will see \nincreasing moisture into the region as dew points surge into the\n60s to as high as the lower/mid 70s across far southwest Arizona \nand southeast California. The increased moisture will lead to heat\nindices upwards of 110-115 degrees with higher values possible\nacross portions of southeast California. Beyond Wednesday, a \nresurgence of the ridge is expected to begin to raise temperatures\nagain with highs drifting 2-3 degrees higher by Friday. Given the\nrestrengthening of the ridge later this week along with higher \nheat indices expected through much of the latter half of the \nweek, the Extreme Heat Warning has been extended through Friday \nevening for most of the area.\n\nMonsoon storm potential for the rest of the week looks to be\nfairly low to at most mid grade with 20-40% chances most days over\nthe eastern Arizona high terrain to 10-20% into the Arizona lower\ndeserts. There are some hints at a potential increase in\nconvective activity maybe by the weekend into early next week, but\nwe do not put much faith in the long range models for monsoon \nactivity. Although, as long as the high center ends up staying \npositioned far enough to our north, it at least seems quite \nplausible any minor easterly wave could march on through \nproviding enhanced chances at some point.",
